y they handle a lot of things.replysaghm 58 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] Yeah, this part also stuck out to me:> Because this wouldn’t be a quick or easy fix, we reached out to the SQLite developers for a professional support contract. This was a great decision. It gave us direct access to their deep expertise and experience, and we had many detailed technical conversations about our architecture and our incidents.They were willing to pay to get help solving the problem, and then pay again to make sure that the problem is easier to avoid in the future! That kind of long-term thinking seems pretty rare nowadays...replypacketlost 18 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] Avery is one of the few people I have enough respect for to look up to.replydevmor 59 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] Their CEO is a very nice and personable guy too. Has given me and others advice on random topics of his interest with no nonsense plenty of times.replyEastSmith 19 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] Started using them like 2 weeks back, happy to see how they work.replycalmingsolitude 51 minutes ago | prev | next [–] Well written post, really enjoyed reading it.> A single Go process exclusively accesses that database, and serves the control plane for those tailnets. This single-writer design is exactly how SQLite is meant to be used.This line led me to believe that the writer and checkpointing logic lived on the same database connection, so I was curious to find out how the data race occurred. However, the bug details on the SQLite page[0] outline that it can only ever occur if there are multiple connections open, so the writer and the checkpointer must have been on different threads.[0] https://sqlite.org/wal.html#the_wal_reset_bugreplybobtheborg 56 minutes ago | prev | next [–] Great read. So glad they took the time to tell this story. (And glad they, as a for profit corporation, took out a support contract with SQLite. I hope they continue to do so even though this problem is resolved.)replyjeffbee 0 minutes ago | prev | next [–] Block device upfuckery layers are powerful against databases. Years ago some colleagues wrote one that provides most of the hazards described by \"Parity Lost and Parity Regained\"[1] to test FoundationDB, which immediately uncovered several flaws in a project that described itself as well-tested. It's easy to do this with all the probing features that Linux (and others) provide today.1: https://www.usenix.org/legacy/event/fast08/tech/full_papers/...replydeclan_roberts 3 minutes ago | prev | next [–] > Now we’re in summer, we’re confident that we’ve found the bug, that we understand it—and more importantly, that we’ve fixed it.This is the feeling I chase as a software engineer. It's the greatest motivator.replyriknos314 35 minutes ago | prev | next [–] > Whenever corruption occurred, we had to stop the control plane process on the shard while we repaired or restored the database. This was painful for tailnets on that shard, because their entire control plane disappeared during that recovery window.Gotta love single points of failure...replykccqzy 27 minutes ago | parent | next [–] What are some solutions to avoid database corruption being single points of failure? I can’t think of any off the top of my head. I don’t think people typically consider database corruption to be a kind of failure common enough to design for, unless you have unusual requirements.replydolmen 59 minutes ago | prev | next [–] Which SQLite driver for Go does Tailscale use?replysethops1 11 minutes ago | parent | next [–] https://github.com/tailscale/sqlitereplyec109685 45 minutes ago | prev | next [–] While technically true as written, it seems to downplay the significance:> The bug is a data race with tight timing constraints. It is unlikely to occur in common use.A large customer did experience this corruption, so it's important for people with tailscale's setup update immediately.> The developers have never been able to reproduce the bug organically and had to add special testing logic to SQLite that deliberately triggers the circumstances of the the bug in order to verify that the issue has been fixed.replyAriarule 25 minutes ago | parent | next [–] Odd not to highlight the sentence where they answer the obvious question \"Why Tailscale in particular?\":> They also explained why we were more likely to hit the bug than other SQLite users: we take manual control of the checkpointing process, and we checkpoint very aggressively. Even a bug triggered by a rare condition was bound to hit us eventually.replypstuart 15 minutes ago | prev | next [–] I imagine the SQLite eschews AI generated code, but using it for testing (vulnerability, performance, etc) would seem like an easy win.I know their proprietary testing framework is their secret sauce so we may never know...replynoncoml 25 minutes ago | prev [–] How come mythos didn’t find this?reply Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact Search:\n=== END PAGE TEXT ===\n\nGoal: Visit Hacker News and:\n1. Identify the top story on the front page (the first article in the list)\n2. Drill into its comments thread\n3. Record the story title (key=story_title) and story URL (key=story_url) in working memory\n4. Record your summary of the top 3 comments as key=top_comments (one paragraph, 2-3 sentences)\n5. Call terminate(finalAnswer=...) with a one-paragraph summary including the story title and a synthesis of what commenters are saying",
